      "target": "q1/b",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            48
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 1 (b)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "Many candidates were able to show that when calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, they bind to troponin causing tropomyosin to move and make the binding site available for the myosin head to bind. Weaker candidates tended to give a short answer or leave this part blank. A few candidates missed out on credit by referring to myosin binding to actin and not more specifically referring to the myosin head binding to actin."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q1.c",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q1/c",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            48
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 1 (c)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "This question required candidates to suggest why muscles remained contracted after death, based on their knowledge of the sliding filament theory. A good response would be to state that little ATP would be formed and consequently cross bridges would not break, leaving the muscle contracted. A minority of candidates were able to achieve full credit."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
